<div class="rg-grid">
  <div class="rg-copy">
    <p class="eyebrow">Resource governance</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Configure resource limits for large files.</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      DOCX, XLSX and PPTX share measured package-expansion limits. The defaults allow
      <strong>128 MiB per part</strong>, <strong>256 MiB across distinct parts read</strong>,
      and <strong>4,096 archive entries</strong> during a package session. Reaching a limit rejects with a structured
      <code>OoxmlResourceLimitError</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="rg-facts">
      <div>
        <strong>Collect usage metrics</strong>
        <span><code>onResourceMetrics</code> captures the initial load independently of debug logging; <code>getResourceMetrics()</code> returns a fresh snapshot after lazy package access.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Inspect debug output</strong>
        <span><code>debug: true</code> prints the same report as one aligned console card. The library never transmits or stores it.</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Use Worker mode</strong>
        <span><code>mode: 'worker'</code> keeps parser and renderer state away from Window. It is opt-in and is not an operating-system process or a strict memory sandbox.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="rg-caveat">
      Raster decode is also governed uniformly. Ordinary browser rasters receive a geometry-weighted
      share of the <strong>128 MiB decoded budget</strong> before source extraction, so each image can
      flow directly into decode. Sources that fit keep their native grid; oversized sources use up to
      a 2x canvas/DPR grid when their share has headroom. If the complete set of display grids exceeds the budget, adaptive mode
      reduces them by one uniform quality ratio. Image-bearing paints for one
      document are serialized so overlapping paints cannot each consume the full budget, while the admitted paint still decodes up to
      two images concurrently. Applications can configure <code>imageResources.decodedByteBudget</code>
      up to 512 MiB, select <code>strategy: 'strict'</code>, or use
      <code>resolution: 'display'</code> to minimize retained raster pixels. The encoded source grid, per-axis and
      per-surface ceilings remain non-disableable. Natural-size consumers, pixel effects that need
      the authored grid, and non-resizable sources remain subject to
      those hard ceilings, so a tiny display request cannot bypass decode-bomb
      protection. These are deterministic admission guards—not exact
      JavaScript, WASM, canvas or GPU accounting—so they bound planned and retained RGBA surfaces but cannot
      guarantee that every device avoids OOM.
      Browser-managed SVG/vector storage has no portable decoded-byte metric; its cache is
      count-bounded and owned object URLs are revoked, but it is outside the RGBA counter.
    </p>
  </div>

  <pre class="rg-code"><code><span class="kw">const</span> viewer = <span class="kw">new</span> DocxViewer(canvas, {'{'}
  resourceLimits: {'{'}
    maxArchiveEntryBytes: <span class="num">128</span> * <span class="num">1024</span> * <span class="num">1024</span>,
    maxTotalInflatedBytes: <span class="num">256</span> * <span class="num">1024</span> * <span class="num">1024</span>,
    maxArchiveEntries: <span class="num">4096</span>,
  {'}'},
  imageResources: {'{'}
    decodedByteBudget: <span class="num">128</span> * <span class="num">1024</span> * <span class="num">1024</span>,
    strategy: <span class="str">'adaptive'</span>,
    resolution: <span class="str">'native-if-fit'</span>,
  {'}'},
  onResourceMetrics(metrics) {'{'}
    analytics.record(<span class="str">'ooxml-resource-usage'</span>, metrics);
  {'}'},
  <span class="cm">// debug: true, // local or staging inspection</span>
  <span class="cm">// mode: 'worker', // optional stronger containment</span>
{'}'});</code></pre>
</div>

